  "text": "umably, we want to find the sum of all positive integer divisors of $20!$ that are not divisible by $10$ and then find the remainder when this sum is divided by $20!$.\n\nLet's start by considering $20!$. The prime factorization of $20!$ is:\n\\[ 20! = 2^{18} \\cdot 3^8 \\cdot 5^3 \\cdot 7^2 \\cdot 11 \\cdot 13 \\cdot 17 \\cdot 19 \\]\n\nA divisor of $20!$ that is not divisible by $10$ must not contain the factor $2$ or the factor $5$. We will exclude these factors from the product and then sum the remaining terms, forming the product for each term that does not contain either factor. \n\nFirst, let's find the number of divisors of $20!$ that are divisible by $5$ (and thus counted once wrongly in not divisible by $10$):\n\\[ n_{5^3} = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{20}{5} \\right\\rfloor \\cdot \\left\\lfloor \\frac{20}{25} \\right\\rfloor \\cdot \\left\\lfloor \\frac{20}{125} \\right\\rfloor = 4 \\]\n\nThere are no divisors divisible by both $2$ and $5$ (as $2 \\neq 5$).\n\n\\[ n_5 = n_{5^1} + n_{5^3} = 8 + 4 = 12 \\]\n\nThe sum of divisors of $n_Z = n_Z \\left( \\frac{(p-1)}{p} \\right)^{18} \\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right)^8 \\left( \\frac{2}{5} \\right)^2 \\left( \\frac{4}{7} \\right) \\left( \\frac{12}{11} \\right) \\left( \\frac{12}{13} \\right) \\left( \\frac{12}{17} \\right) \\left( \\frac{12}{19} \\right) \\]\n\nSo our final answer is $4$. The remainder when $4$ is divided by $20!$ is simply $4$. So, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}\n\\]",
